{=============================================================================
  von Karman viscous pump (rotating disk)
  CoolSolve Library model CSL-0338
 -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Three coupled ODEs for the laminar flow over an infinite rotating disk
  (White, Viscous Fluid Flow, 2005, p. 162), integrated from x = 0 to 10.
  The wall slopes f`_0 and g`_0 are shooting parameters; Obj measures
  f and g at x = 10. Dimensionless. Details in README.md.

  Status : blocked. Prime names are CS-GAP-NAME-PRIME; -g^2 is read as
           (-g)^2 (CS-BUG-UNARY-POW). The runnable variant renames the
           derivatives and writes -(g^2).
  Source : ~/Nextcloud/thermo_models/EES_library/von Karman.EES (EES X8.807).
           (c) F-Chart Software / S. A. Klein, MIT via ees-examples.
=============================================================================}

"The differential equations"
f``=-g^2+f^2+f`*h
g``=2*f*g+h*g`
h`=-2*f

"Initial boundary conditions"
f_0=0
g_0=1
h_0=0

step=0 "force automatic step size adjustment"

"Numerical solution using Integral function"
f`=f`_0+integral(f``,x,0,10,step)
f=f_0+integral(f`,x,0,10,step)
g`=g`_0+integral(g``,x,0,10,step)
g=g_0+integral(g`,x,0,10,step)
h=h_0+integral(h`,x,0,10,step)

"Specified values  at the end condition"
f_inf_exact=0
g_inf_exact=0

"Objective function - minimize to determine the values of f`_0 and g`_0"
Obj=abs(g-g_inf_exact)+abs(f -f_inf_exact)

"Shooting parameters: the slopes at the origin, set to the values that
reproduce the stored EES solution (EES minimises Obj over them, with the ranges
0 to 1 for f`_0 and -1 to 0 for g`_0)"
f`_0=0.510226344786
g`_0=-0.615925074583

"The exact result, provided in White, is f`_0=0.5102 and g`_0=-0.6159"
